## 3. Local semantics (what this repository's agent-teams actually is)

### 3.1 Default-on mechanical session-start policy (the thing to de-default)
`lib/session-start.js:1-40` states the module contract: *"Prompt-level guidance (...) is not enough - a session
can quietly run solo"*; the hook is the `agent/pre-step` waterfall, installed `global + prepend`
(`:229-269`), **settle-once per agent** (`:242-244`). Qualification (`:71-84`): top-level sessions only
(`header.parentSession` must be absent, so members/subagents/workflow workers never qualify) and the preset
allow-list (`presets: []` = every session). `mode: 'auto'` calls `provisionSessionTeam` (`:123-168`), which
reuses the same `initializeProfileTeam` path as `agent_teams_create`, with `staged: true`, then injects the
startup notice (`:174-187`); any provisioning error degrades to the `instruct` notice (`:194-203`) rather than
breaking the step. `mode: 'instruct'` injects only the notice; `mode: 'off'` does nothing.
Schema: `lib/index.js:87-98` — **`sessionTeamPolicy` defaults to `{ mode: 'off' }`**. The bundle enables it:
`packages/mpd-bundle/cordis.patch.yml:204-224` (`mode: auto`, `profile: mpd`, `presets: [mpd]`,
`name: MPD Default`, `approval: required`). Documented in `packages/mpd-bundle/README.md:22-42` and asserted by
`skills/dsh-qa/scripts/session-start-team.mjs:85,87,129,133` and `scripts/install-profile.mjs:261-262`.

### 3.2 The manual entry (must not change)
`lib/command.js:3,5,26`: `AGENT_TEAMS_COMMAND = 'agent-teams'`, `GESTURE = /^\/agent-teams(?=$|[\t\n\r ])/u`,
matched at `:26`; registered at `lib/index.js:233-248` (slash command via `ctx.inject(['commands'])` + the
plain-text gesture boundary for surfaces without command adjudication), with `slashCommand` defaulting true.
The tool-level entry is `agent_teams_create`.

### 3.3 The DAG is the shared task list, and it is human-reviewable
`lib/scheduler.js:1-13`: an event-driven scheduler; every `agent/status` idle edge and every task-graph
mutation attempts one atomic claim and wakes a durable member (`:491-495`). Dependencies are
`dependencies: string[]`; completed ancestors are collected **topologically** and injected into the assignment
prompt with per-item 2 000-char and total 12 000-char caps (`:17-20`, `:36-84`); a cycle prunes only that
branch (a warning, `:43`). A member that becomes idle while owning an open attempt is **parked** until an
explicit captain reassignment; automatic retry is reserved for cold recovery when the durable owner is no
longer resident (`:440-481`).

### 3.4 The quality plane is stronger than upstream's doctrine
`lib/quality-gates.js:7-27` defines kinds requirements/implementation/verification/review/repair/integration,
the review policy (requirements 1..4 rounds, code max 3, max 2 repair attempts) and the default review
acceptance. `validateCreateTask` (`:391`) is a create-time contract/contradiction gate. `planQualityFollowUp`
(`:659`) opens repair + the next review automatically. `canDeclareDelivery` (`:769-812`) blocks delivery when a
review/requirements task completed without `verdict=pass`, when a failed quality task has no follow-up repair,
when a completed implementation has no passing review, and when any `changedPaths` entry falls outside the
contract's `inScope`/`outOfScope`. `buildCoverageMatrix` (`:754-768`) maps goal items to covering tasks.

### 3.5 A separate local ultrawork plane already exists
`packages/mpd-ulw-plugin/README.md:4-25`: `mpd_ultrawork` / `mpd_ulw` is a fixed-policy engine adapted from
upstream ultrawork at base `8c57e46` (discovery waves, PIN->RED->GREEN->SURFACE->CLEAN per criterion, plan gate,
verification gate, final ledger, optional hyperplan wave). It runs on the **DSH subagent seam**, not on
agent-teams — so this repo already contains *two* orchestration planes with no router between them.

### 3.6 The DSH `workflow` tool is NOT upstream's workflow/dag tool
The preset mounts `tool-workflow` (`presets/mpd/agent.cordis.yml:349-355`, `workflowEngine: true` at `:305`).
Its script hook surface is `agent()` / `pipeline()` / `parallel()` / `phase()` / `log()` / `args` with a JSON
return. It has **no** `run_id`, no snapshot/lifecycle verbs, no `retry`/`amend`/`send`, no durable journal, no
eval-cell SDK, and a throwing stage resolves that **item** to null rather than failing the run. Upstream's
`tool.workflow({action})` is the dag tool itself. Same name, different machine.

### 3.7 The local corpus does not contain mass-ulw, yet two files point at it
`skills/*/SKILL.md` has 19 entries and no mass-ulw. But `skills/ulw-execute/SKILL.md:103` instructs: *"Read the
`mass-ulw` skill's `SKILL.md` and `references/planning.md` IN FULL before defining any graph"*, and
`skills/frontend/references/design/print-paged-media.md:76` defers to "the `mass-ulw` verification wave" — a
dangling cross-reference in the shipped corpus today. The adopt/adapt/skip verdict is t4/t8 scope.

